1	// 06 · Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio: the basics everyone should know.
2	
3	module.exports = {
4	  num: 6,
5	  slug: "acute-chronic-workload-ratio",
6	  title: "Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio",
7	  category: "Recovery & Load Management",
8	  palette: "forest",
9	  hero: { layout: "ratio-scale-band", concept: "A single ratio scale from 0.5 to 2.0: the familiar 'close to usual' band and the spike zone above 1.5" },
10	  oneIdea: "Sudden spikes in load are the risk. The ratio is a way to spot them, not a verdict.",
11	  question: "Where in our calendar does load jump without anyone planning it?",
12	  caveats: `ACWR is contested. Present 0.8-1.3 and 1.5 as where the early studies drew lines, not as validated thresholds. If someone asks "should we stop using it?": the defensible position is that it is a reasonable flag for big week-to-week jumps, read alongside the athlete and the plan, and never an injury predictor on its own.`,
13	  sources: [
14	    { id: "gabbett2016", cite: "Gabbett TJ. The training-injury prevention paradox: should athletes be training smarter and harder? Br J Sports Med. 2016;50(5):273-280. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2015-095788", grade: "influential narrative review; thresholds are descriptive, not validated", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Williams 2017)", supports: "The 0.8-1.3 'sweet spot' and the 1.5 'danger zone' framing." },
15	    { id: "hulin2016", cite: "Hulin BT, Gabbett TJ, Lawson DW, Caputi P, Sampson JA. The acute:chronic workload ratio predicts injury: high chronic workload may decrease injury risk in elite rugby league players. Br J Sports Med. 2016;50(4):231-236. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2015-094817", grade: "single-cohort observational study", found: "Zotero", supports: "Players with high chronic load coped with moderate ratios; injury risk rose with spikes in acute load." },
16	    { id: "impellizzeri2020", cite: "Impellizzeri FM, Ward P, Coutts AJ, Bornn L, McCall A. Training load and injury part 2: questionable research practices hijack the truth and mislead well-intentioned clinicians. J Orthop Sports Phys Ther. 2020;50(10):577-584. doi:10.2519/jospt.2020.9211", grade: "methodological critique", found: "Zotero", supports: "Results across ACWR studies are 'inconsistent and often confusing' (p. 578)." },
17	    { id: "windt2019", cite: "Windt J, Gabbett TJ. Is it all for naught? What does mathematical coupling mean for acute:chronic workload ratios? Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(16):988-990. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2017-098925", grade: "methodological commentary", found: "Zotero", supports: "The coupled ratio shares data between numerator and denominator, so part of the relationship comes from the arithmetic." },
18	  ],
19	  slides: [
20	    {
21	      role: "hook", time: 45,
22	      notes: `Open on the number. If you have been around load monitoring in the last ten years, you have heard "1.5". It comes from the acute:chronic workload ratio: this week's load divided by what the athlete has averaged over the last month or so.
23	
24	The 2016 review that popularised it called 0.8 to 1.3 a "sweet spot" and 1.5 and above a "danger zone". That framing travelled fast, into apps, dashboards and staff meetings. Today is about what is worth keeping from it.`,
25	      build(s) {
26	        s.figure("1.5", { x: 0.7, y: 1.05, w: 5.6, h: 4.4, size: 280, refs: ["gabbett2016"] });
27	        s.headline("The most quoted number in load monitoring.", { x: 6.9, y: 1.9, w: 5.7, h: 1.6, size: 40 });
28	        s.lead("It compares this week's load with what an athlete is used to. Above 1.5 became shorthand for danger.", { x: 6.9, y: 3.75, w: 5.5, h: 1.6, size: 22 });
29	        s.source("Gabbett, Br J Sports Med, 2016", { x: 6.9, y: 5.95, w: 5.5, refs: ["gabbett2016"] });
30	      },
31	    },
32	    {
33	      role: "problem", time: 60,
34	      notes: `Here is the problem. What spread was the simple version: keep everyone between 0.8 and 1.3 and injuries go down.
35	
36	What came after was messier. Reviews of the studies found the results inconsistent and often confusing. And the way the ratio is usually calculated puts this week inside the monthly average too, so part of the relationship is just arithmetic. Researchers still argue about how much that matters.
37	
38	None of this means load does not matter. It means a single ratio is not a verdict on an athlete.`,
39	      build(s) {
40	        s.headline("The number got simple. The science didn't.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
41	        s.label("What spread", { x: 0.75, y: 2.75, w: 5.2 });
42	        s.text(0.75, 3.2, 5.2, 2.2, "Keep the ratio between 0.8 and 1.3 and injuries go down.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.15 });
43	        s.line(6.62, 2.8, 6.62, 5.6, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
44	        s.label("What later work found", { x: 7.1, y: 2.75, w: 5.4 });
45	        s.text(7.1, 3.2, 5.45, 2.4, "Results across studies are inconsistent, and part of the link comes from the maths of the ratio itself.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.15 });
46	        s.source("Gabbett 2016 · Impellizzeri et al. 2020 · Windt & Gabbett 2019", { refs: ["gabbett2016", "impellizzeri2020", "windt2019"] });
47	      },
48	    },
49	    {
50	      role: "insight", time: 75,
51	      notes: `What holds up is the idea underneath the number. Athletes tolerate hard work they have built up to. What gets them in trouble is a sudden jump well beyond what they are used to.
52	
