// 06 · Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio: the basics everyone should know.

module.exports = {
  num: 6,
  slug: "acute-chronic-workload-ratio",
  title: "Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio",
  category: "Recovery & Load Management",
  palette: "forest",
  hero: { layout: "ratio-scale-band", concept: "A single ratio scale from 0.5 to 2.0: the familiar 'close to usual' band and the spike zone above 1.5" },
  oneIdea: "Sudden spikes in load are the risk. The ratio is a way to spot them, not a verdict.",
  question: "Where in our calendar does load jump without anyone planning it?",
  caveats: `ACWR is contested. Present 0.8-1.3 and 1.5 as where the early studies drew lines, not as validated thresholds. If someone asks "should we stop using it?": the defensible position is that it is a reasonable flag for big week-to-week jumps, read alongside the athlete and the plan, and never an injury predictor on its own.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "gabbett2016", cite: "Gabbett TJ. The training-injury prevention paradox: should athletes be training smarter and harder? Br J Sports Med. 2016;50(5):273-280. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2015-095788", grade: "influential narrative review; thresholds are descriptive, not validated", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Williams 2017)", supports: "The 0.8-1.3 'sweet spot' and the 1.5 'danger zone' framing." },
    { id: "hulin2016", cite: "Hulin BT, Gabbett TJ, Lawson DW, Caputi P, Sampson JA. The acute:chronic workload ratio predicts injury: high chronic workload may decrease injury risk in elite rugby league players. Br J Sports Med. 2016;50(4):231-236. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2015-094817", grade: "single-cohort observational study", found: "Zotero", supports: "Players with high chronic load coped with moderate ratios; injury risk rose with spikes in acute load." },
    { id: "impellizzeri2020", cite: "Impellizzeri FM, Ward P, Coutts AJ, Bornn L, McCall A. Training load and injury part 2: questionable research practices hijack the truth and mislead well-intentioned clinicians. J Orthop Sports Phys Ther. 2020;50(10):577-584. doi:10.2519/jospt.2020.9211", grade: "methodological critique", found: "Zotero", supports: "Results across ACWR studies are 'inconsistent and often confusing' (p. 578)." },
    { id: "windt2019", cite: "Windt J, Gabbett TJ. Is it all for naught? What does mathematical coupling mean for acute:chronic workload ratios? Br J Sports Med. 2019;53(16):988-990. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2017-098925", grade: "methodological commentary", found: "Zotero", supports: "The coupled ratio shares data between numerator and denominator, so part of the relationship comes from the arithmetic." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Open on the number. If you have been around load monitoring in the last ten years, you have heard "1.5". It comes from the acute:chronic workload ratio: this week's load divided by what the athlete has averaged over the last month or so.

The 2016 review that popularised it called 0.8 to 1.3 a "sweet spot" and 1.5 and above a "danger zone". That framing travelled fast, into apps, dashboards and staff meetings. Today is about what is worth keeping from it.`,
      build(s) {
        s.figure("1.5", { x: 0.7, y: 1.05, w: 5.6, h: 4.4, size: 280, refs: ["gabbett2016"] });
        s.headline("The most quoted number in load monitoring.", { x: 6.9, y: 1.9, w: 5.7, h: 1.6, size: 40 });
        s.lead("It compares this week's load with what an athlete is used to. Above 1.5 became shorthand for danger.", { x: 6.9, y: 3.75, w: 5.5, h: 1.6, size: 22 });
        s.source("Gabbett, Br J Sports Med, 2016", { x: 6.9, y: 5.95, w: 5.5, refs: ["gabbett2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is the problem. What spread was the simple version: keep everyone between 0.8 and 1.3 and injuries go down.

What came after was messier. Reviews of the studies found the results inconsistent and often confusing. And the way the ratio is usually calculated puts this week inside the monthly average too, so part of the relationship is just arithmetic. Researchers still argue about how much that matters.

None of this means load does not matter. It means a single ratio is not a verdict on an athlete.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The number got simple. The science didn't.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.label("What spread", { x: 0.75, y: 2.75, w: 5.2 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.2, 5.2, 2.2, "Keep the ratio between 0.8 and 1.3 and injuries go down.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.line(6.62, 2.8, 6.62, 5.6, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("What later work found", { x: 7.1, y: 2.75, w: 5.4 });
        s.text(7.1, 3.2, 5.45, 2.4, "Results across studies are inconsistent, and part of the link comes from the maths of the ratio itself.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.source("Gabbett 2016 · Impellizzeri et al. 2020 · Windt & Gabbett 2019", { refs: ["gabbett2016", "impellizzeri2020", "windt2019"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `What holds up is the idea underneath the number. Athletes tolerate hard work they have built up to. What gets them in trouble is a sudden jump well beyond what they are used to.

Read the scale left to right. 1.0 means this week looks like a normal week for that athlete. The shaded band is "close to usual". Past 1.5 is a spike: half again more than they are prepared for. In the rugby league data, players with a solid base handled ordinary weeks well, and their risk rose with spikes.

So the ratio is a way to spot spikes. That is its job, and that is where its job ends.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("What holds up: sudden spikes are the risk, not hard work.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.6, h: 1.5, size: 40 });
        const X = r => 1.25 + (r - 0.5) * 7.233;
        const y = 4.6;
        s.rect(X(0.8), y - 0.62, X(1.3) - X(0.8), 0.78, { fill: "soft" });
        s.line(X(0.5), y, X(2.0), y, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
        s.line(X(1.5), y, X(2.0), y, { color: "accent", w: 7 });
        [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0].forEach(r => {
          s.line(X(r), y - 0.14, X(r), y + 0.14, { color: r === 1.5 ? "accent" : "muted", w: 1.5 });
          s.text(X(r) - 0.6, y + 0.3, 1.2, 0.45, r.toFixed(1), { font: "cond", size: 22, align: "center", color: r === 1.5 ? "accent" : "ink" });
        });
        s.text(X(0.8), y - 1.2, X(1.3) - X(0.8), 0.45, "0.8–1.3  close to usual", { font: "cond", size: 18, align: "center", color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(1.5), y - 1.2, X(2.0) - X(1.5), 0.45, "1.5 and up  a spike", { font: "condBold", size: 20, color: "accent" });
        s.text(X(0.5), y + 0.95, 4, 0.4, "Less than usual", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(2.0) - 4, y + 0.95, 4, 0.4, "More than usual", { font: "light", size: 16, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.source("Lines as drawn in Gabbett 2016; spike finding from Hulin et al. 2016", { refs: ["gabbett2016", "hulin2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So how do we use it here? As a question, not a red light.

First, know where the jumps live: the first week back from a break, the first days of camp, the return from an injury. Those are predictable.

Second, when the number jumps, ask why. Was it planned, or did it creep up through extra sessions nobody added up?

Third, any change to a plan goes through the people who own it: the sport coach, and Athletic Medicine for anyone in rehab or return to play. The number starts the conversation. It never ends it.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Use it as a question, not a red light.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const cols = [
          ["1", "Know where jumps live: the week back from a break, the start of camp, a return from injury."],
          ["2", "When the number jumps, ask whether it was planned or crept up."],
          ["3", "Check with the sport coach and Athletic Medicine before a plan changes."],
        ];
        cols.forEach(([k, t], i) => {
          const x = 0.75 + i * 4.0;
          s.line(x, 2.75, x + 3.55, 2.75, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(x, 2.95, 1.5, 1.2, k, { font: "condLight", size: 72, color: "accent", lineH: 0.9 });
          s.text(x, 4.1, 3.55, 2.1, t, { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.2 });
        });
        s.cite("hulin2016");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `One line to take away: ramp, don't spike.

Most spikes are on the calendar weeks before they happen. If we plan those weeks on purpose, we get the benefit of hard work without the jump.

Question for the room: where in our calendar does load jump without anyone planning it? Give people a moment; the answers are usually specific and useful.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Ramp, don't spike.", { y: 1.35, w: 11.5, h: 1.8, size: 104 });
        s.lead("Most spikes are on the calendar weeks before they happen. Plan those weeks on purpose.", { y: 3.5, w: 8.5, h: 1.2, size: 24 });
        s.line(0.75, 4.95, 1.75, 4.95, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.15, 11.8, 1.0, "Where in our calendar does load jump without anyone planning it?", { font: "medium", size: 26, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

